## 3. Document sectioning (todo step 1)

| Approach | What | Cost | Fit |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Heuristic on page text** | regex/keyword scan of each page's first lines for system headers (`244.xx LAyy ...`, `Seite n / m`), TOC, chapter keywords | ~free, deterministic | the PDF literally prints the system on every page — highest precision signal available |
| **Layout-analysis model** | DocLayNet-trained detectors (YOLO-family, e.g. `hantian/yolo-doclaynet`, docling LayoutModel); 11 classes (tables, text, captions...) | torch + ultralytics dep | gives table/page-type maps; useful later for table-region hints, not needed for boundaries |
| **LLM sectioning** | feed per-page summaries (or TOC) to LLM, get the `json sections` from todo | cheap with small context | good *confirmation* layer; hallucination risk if used blind on raw pages |
| Marker/docling built-in chunkers | structure-aware Markdown + sections | heavy deps | redundant here |

**Recommendation:** two-phase hybrid: (a) script scans page texts for system-header patterns ->
candidate boundaries; (b) LLM receives one-line-per-page digests and emits the todo's
`{number_of_sections, sections[{page_from, page_to, desc}]}` JSON, constrained to agree with (a)
where (a) is confident. Deterministic pass is the anchor; LLM writes the human-readable `desc` and
fixes ambiguous pages. No layout model in v1; revisit DocLayNet/YOLO only if table detection needs
region hints.

