## 5. Entity matching (positions vs `catalog.csv`)

The matching is **cross-lingual** (German tender text vs English catalog descriptions) and
dimension-heavy (`DN100`, `400x300x1250`) - a pure string-similarity approach is not enough.

| Tool | What | License | Fit |
|---|---|---|---|
| `difflib` (stdlib) | SequenceMatcher ratios | stdlib | zero-dep baseline; good enough for exact-ish duplicates |
| **rapidfuzz** | C++ fuzz metrics (`token_set_ratio`, `WRatio`, `jaro_winkler`), `process.extract`/`cdist` (r2: all confirmed in README; cdist is the fast path) | MIT (3.14.6, 2026-08-30; r4-reconfirmed) | **[EXEC r3]** installed + run on the golden set: `token_set_ratio` ranks the correct product **#1 in 6/6** cases (r4: extended to **22/22**, Round 4 §1); **WRatio fails 3/6** (score ties at 86) -> use token_set_ratio only; zero deps, 12 MB |
| **sentence-transformers + multilingual embeddings** (`intfloat/multilingual-e5-small` **MIT** or `paraphrase-multilingual-MiniLM-L12-v2` **Apache-2.0**, both licenses r2-confirmed; both 384-d) | 384-d vectors, cosine top-k; e5 requires `"query: "`/`"passage: "` input prefixes (r2-confirmed — easy to forget, silently degrades matching) | Apache/MIT models, but torch dep | **[DOCS]** optional tier; r3 quantified the need precisely: raw German vs English catalog tops out at **27-33** token_set — useless; but if extraction emits English descriptions (as the golden CSV does), embeddings are unnecessary for this catalog |
| Cross-encoder reranker (ms-marco MiniLM) | joint scoring of (position, candidate) | Apache | **[DOCS]** unnecessary at catalog size 56 |
| Attribute/dimension parse | numeric **dimension profile**: multiset of DN/WxH/WxHxL tokens (x-joined digits included, EI30/EI90 fire ratings excluded) | stdlib | **[EXEC r4 — load-bearing]**: across all 22 golden rows the tightest top-1 margin is **1.6 pts** (LV0012: 91.3 correct vs 89.7 wrong) and P-SIL-001 vs dimension-sibling P-SIL-004 is 5.6 pts (100 vs 94.4) — no score-only cut separates these; the profile veto decides. It also rescues the desc+model and difflib variants from 21/22 and 20/22 back to 22/22 (Round 4 §1) |

**Recommendation (r4-rewritten; supersedes the r2 weighted-blend formula AND the r3 band-flag
policy — both refuted by execution, Round 4 §1):** score = `token_set_ratio(position.description,
catalog.description)` — **descriptions only**, never `desc+model` concatenation (it drops LV0012
to a wrong product: wrong 78.8 vs correct 72.4). Apply a three-part veto first: equal `unit`,
equal `category`, equal numeric **dimension profiles** (multiset of DN/WxH/WxHxL tokens; EIxx
ratings excluded); top-1 of the survivors wins. rapidfuzz is a pinned **required** dep (MIT, zero
deps, 12 MB); `difflib` is a degraded fallback, not an equal alternative (20/22 raw on golden).
The raw score drives ONLY whether the LLM tie-break runs — **never the review flag**: golden flags
are source-driven (`supplier_quote_required=true` in catalog.csv ⇒ review_required=true,
status=supplier_quote_required, price_source=supplier_package, match_confidence=0.72; catalog row
⇒ 0.92 / false / calculated — reproduced 22/22, Round 4 §2). Manufacturer/model hints are
tie-break evidence and at most a small bonus, not input text (catalog manufacturers are fictional
Demo*, so a name-match bonus is unmeasurable here). The English-description contract at
extraction is what makes this work (Round 3 §4); embeddings only if extraction ever stays German
(raw German vs English catalog = 27-33 token_set, noise). WRatio stays dropped (3/6 r3 failures
from 86-ties).

## 6. Comparator / verification (todo step 3's `<comparator agent>`)

| Pattern | What | Cost | Fit |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Quote-anchored check** | extractor must return `quote_source_text`; verifier confirms fuzzy presence in the section's page text and that qty/unit appear in it | 1 extra cheap call or even rapidfuzz-only | **[EXEC r3 — design corrected]** highest-value pattern, BUT the quote must be the **verbatim German source line**, not the translated English description: `partial_ratio(english_desc, german_page)` scores **35-43 — indistinguishable from a fake quote (33-39)**; `partial_ratio(german_verbatim, page)` = **100** on all 6 real quotes vs 33-37 on fakes. Schema must mandate `quote_source_text` = exact German substring |
| **Self-consistency** | k samples at temperature, majority vote per field (esp. quantity) | k x tokens | +3-18 pts on reasoning tasks (Wang et al.); use only for low-confidence rows |
| **NLI entailment** | `cross-encoder/nli-deberta-v3-base` (0.2 B, Apache-2.0): premise=page text, hypothesis=extracted row; entailment/neutral/contradiction | small model, torch dep | semantic overlap check; German-English mismatch hurts (model is English-centric) |
| **Dual-model cross-check** | extractor LLM A, checker LLM B ("does position X exist on page Y? answer with the line") | 2 x tokens | catches A-specific hallucinations; different LoRA or higher temp for B |
| Deterministic validators | quantity > 0, unit in {pc,m,m2,h,LS,system}, DN parse consistent with description | free | always on; gate before any LLM check |

**Recommendation:** layer it: (1) deterministic validators; (2) quote-anchor verification with
rapidfuzz (`partial_ratio` of quote against page text >= ~90) - no model needed; (3) LLM comparator
agent (same local server, no LoRA or the base model) for rows failing or missing quotes; (4)
self-consistency (k=3) only for `review_required` quantity conflicts. Skip NLI in v1 (torch dep +
language mismatch); the quote-anchor method is cheaper and more explainable in the review UI.

