## How the input-processing LoRA plugs in

`web_ui` Settings already persists, per task ("Input processing", "Productivity factors",
"Win-ratio prediction"): the selected dated LoRA version, the base model, and models/loras/cache
paths (`ui_settings.json`; only versions trained for the current base model are selectable).
`_1_AI_InputProcessing` should:

1. read `ui_settings.json` (path passed via env var, e.g. `AUTOTENDER_UI_SETTINGS`, mirroring the
   existing `AUTOTENDER_CALC_OUT`/`AUTOTENDER_FACTORS` pattern) and resolve
   `<loras_dir>/input_processing_<date>.gguf`;
2. start (or connect to) **one** `llama-server --model <base>.gguf --lora <lora>.gguf
   --lora-init-without-apply` — r2-verified upgrade over round 1: adapters load at scale 0 and are
   selected **per request** via the `lora: [{"id": 0, "scale": 1.0}]` body field (or switched
   globally via `POST /lora-adapters`). The comparator agent calls the same server with
   `"lora": []` (plain base model) in the same process — no second server, no restart;
3. use the OpenAI-compatible endpoint for sectioning-confirmation, per-section extraction agents
   and the comparator; `none` LoRA = scale-0 request, exactly what the UI's `none` option means;
4. when the user switches LoRA version in Settings, the module issues a `POST /lora-adapters`
   (or changes the per-request field) — the round-1 "restart the server" step is no longer needed.
   Perf caveat from the README: requests with different LoRA configurations are not batched
   together, so group extraction calls (adapter on) separately from comparator calls (adapter off).

Note the training-side contract: adapters must be exported/converted to GGUF for llama.cpp runtime
loading (HF safetensors LoRAs need `convert_lora_to_gguf.py`). If training stays on torch/HF,
vLLM with `--enable-lora` is the GPU alternative that consumes HF adapters directly.

## Risks

- **Privacy:** any hosted-API mode sends a client tender off-machine - must stay opt-in and labeled.
- **AGPL trap:** PyMuPDF would force AGPL on the whole tool (r2: AGPL-3.0 re-confirmed via GitHub
  license API); stick to pdfplumber/pypdf. Note marker's *weights* are OpenRAIL-M — commercial use
  beyond the <$5M-startup exemption is paid; another reason it stays out.
- **Table geometry variance (r3: RESOLVED into a recipe):** the naive knob set
  (`vertical_strategy:"text"` etc.) was executed and **fails** on this PDF (mid-word splits,
  collapsed columns, dropped qty). The working recipe is: default `lines` strategy on the ruled
  header to read the true column x-edges once per document, then `extract_words` bucketed by
  x-midpoint into those columns, rows grouped by `round(top/4)` — recovered 7/7 priced items on
  p78 and all golden rows on p79/80 (Round 3 §3). Remaining per-page work: footer/header crop
  bounds and multi-line block assembly.
- **German/English gap (r3: quantified):** raw German item text vs English catalog = **27-33**
  token_set (noise). The pipeline only works because extraction emits English normalized
  descriptions (as the golden CSV does). If extraction ever stays German, the e5 embeddings tier
  stops being optional.
- **Block assembly + units (r3: new, discovered):** qty/unit attach to the **last** physical line
  of an item block ("Druckverlust 30Pa | Stk | 4" closes the Schalldämpfer block), and body
  dimensions are in **cm** ("B x H x L 40 x 30 x 125 cm") while golden+catalog use **mm**
  (400x300x1250; the Typ string even encodes them directly: `MS-F/300x400x1250/1x230/P`). A
  deterministic cm->mm + DN(WxH) normalization post-pass is required, and row assembly must walk
  upward from qty lines to the item title.
- **Hallucinated positions/quantities:** mitigated by quote-anchoring + comparator + validators;
  residual risk lands in `review_required`, which the UI already supports.
- **Long-context degradation** ("lost in the middle"): avoided by sectioning, but keep sections
  under ~30-40 pages so per-section prompts stay far from the context ceiling.
- **Dependency creep (r3: measured):** pdfplumber 0.11.10 alone brings **7 transitive packages**
  (~36 MB installed; pdfminer.six -> cryptography -> cffi -> pycparser, plus pypdfium2 and a
  Pillow 12.0.0->12.3.0 upgrade) — acceptable but no longer "tiny"; rapidfuzz is the clean one
  (zero deps, 12 MB). Every torch-based option (docling, marker, sentence-transformers, NLI)
  stays deferred to optional tiers to preserve the stdlib-first repo convention.
- **SearxNG (r3: re-probed):** service answers HTTP 200 in ~3 s but brave + google cse are
  "Suspended: too many requests" and duckduckgo times out — only the wikipedia engine returns.
  Pace queries, prefer direct primary fetches (PyPI/GitHub/HF APIs).
- **Dev-box inference gap (r3: new):** no llama-server/ollama/vllm installed, ports closed, and
  the 4 GB GTX 1050 Ti / 14 GB RAM cannot host the recommended GGUFs — v1 development either
  uses the opt-in hosted API or a 1-3 B smoke-test model; plan the hardware or accept the
  confidentiality tradeoff explicitly.

